--TEST--
`Core\Process\Handle::kill` ends a child that was still reading, `wait` answers the status that killing it produced, and the handle afterwards refuses a write and reads its stream as ended — `rule:core-classes/process-spawn`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other side of a handle from the streaming case: a program that decides it
// does not want the rest of what a child would have said. Three things have to
// hold together for `kill` to be worth having, and asserting one of them alone
// passes against a member that only pretended — a `kill` that signalled nothing
// would still leave `wait` answering *a* status, and a `wait` that never reaped
// would still let the two reads below answer `null`.
//
// The status itself is deliberately not named. A signal has no exit code on the
// platforms that have signals, so the child answers `-1` there and the number
// the system chose for a terminated process elsewhere; what is common to both,
// and what a caller actually asks, is that it is not the `0` a child that
// finished its work would have reported.

int $started = 0;
int $absent = 0;
int $killed = 0;
string $wrote = "<nothing ran>";
string $ended = "<nothing ran>";

try {
    // A child that reads to the end of its input, started and then never given
    // one: without the kill it would sit there until `wait` closed its stdin.
    Core\Process\Handle $child = Core\Process::spawn("/bin/sh", ["-c", "cat"]);
    $started += 1;
    $child->writeStdin("never answered" as bytes);
    $child->kill();
    Core\Process\Result $done = $child->wait();
    if ($done->exitCode() != 0) {
        $killed += 1;
    }

    // A write into a child that is gone is the operating system's refusal and
    // not a silent success: `wait` closed this end, so there is nothing left
    // for the octets to go into.
    try {
        $child->writeStdin("too late" as bytes);
        $wrote = "accepted";
    } catch (IOError $gone) {
        $wrote = "refused";
    }

    // And *both* streams read as ended rather than as an error, which is the
    // one answer a read gives that is neither data nor a failure. Both,
    // because `wait` drained them separately and a member that left one of
    // them behind would answer a chunk here on the one it forgot.
    ?bytes $afterOut = $child->readStdout();
    ?bytes $afterErr = $child->readStderr();
    $ended = "more";
    if ($afterOut == null && $afterErr == null) {
        $ended = "end";
    }
} catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
    $absent += 1;
}

try {
    Core\Process\Handle $childOnWindows = Core\Process::spawn("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "sort"]);
    $started += 1;
    $childOnWindows->writeStdin("never answered" as bytes);
    $childOnWindows->kill();
    Core\Process\Result $doneOnWindows = $childOnWindows->wait();
    if ($doneOnWindows->exitCode() != 0) {
        $killed += 1;
    }

    try {
        $childOnWindows->writeStdin("too late" as bytes);
        $wrote = "accepted";
    } catch (IOError $goneOnWindows) {
        $wrote = "refused";
    }

    ?bytes $afterOutOnWindows = $childOnWindows->readStdout();
    ?bytes $afterErrOnWindows = $childOnWindows->readStderr();
    $ended = "more";
    if ($afterOutOnWindows == null && $afterErrOnWindows == null) {
        $ended = "end";
    }
} catch (IOError $notWindows) {
    $absent += 1;
}

echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "absent=", $absent, "\n";
echo "killed=", $killed, "\n";
echo "write after=", $wrote, "\n";
echo "read after=", $ended, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true`, not a list of roots, for the reason
# `process-spawn-streams-a-childs-output-into-the-program.nvst` states.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
started=1
absent=1
killed=1
write after=refused
read after=end
